  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the Backend**\n\nI\'ve just been thinking about building the backend with Express. I need to create `server.ts` to get everything going, and specifically, to parse those markdown documents. Instead of grabbing a pre-made library, I think I\'ll write a simple parser directly in `server.ts`. This seems like the right move, so I\'m designing it now.\n\n\n**Parsing Markdown Files**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how `server.ts` will actually read and parse the markdown files. I\'ve realized I need the `fs` and `path` modules. I\'ve designed a function to parse the frontmatter, the part with the key-value pairs at the beginning. It\'s relatively simple: the files start and end with `---`, and in between are key-value pairs. I\'ve started implementing the TypeScript function in `server.ts` to handle this parsing, taking care to define the `Post` interface, as well.\n\n\n**Refining File Parsing**\n\nI\'ve got the markdown parsing function nailed down in `server.ts`. It\'s robust and correctly typed, handling frontmatter and content with no issues. I\'m focusing on handling metadata defaults now, which is a key part of our design. Now, I\'m thinking about whether we also need list and details API endpoints.\n\n\n**Expanding API Endpoints**\n\nI\'m now thinking about API endpoints. I need a `/posts` endpoint that returns post metadata, and a `/posts/:id` endpoint for full content. I want to build a fully interactive blog, so I\'m considering endpoints for creating, editing, and deleting posts, as well as submitting comments. I\'m imagining features like filtering, searching, comments, bookmarks, and theme switching to make this a truly engaging news blog.\n\n\n**Adding Interactive Features**\n\nI\'m now considering interactive features. Users need to toggle layouts and subscribe to newsletters, with subscriptions stored in memory. The core focus is a user-friendly modal to publish new posts, saving them locally or in a cache. We must adhere strictly to user intent, reading blog posts from a local markdown folder. We\'re keeping the design clean with essential interactive elements, like search, to avoid over-engineering. Mobile optimization is critical, with a focus on Core Web Vitals and a clean, readable layout.\n\n\n**Integrating Vite Server**\n\nI\'m now integrating Vite. I\'m focusing on the `server.ts` code structure and how Vite will serve the client-side SPA in development. I\'m thinking about including `/filter` and a mobile-vitals tracker. Also, I\'m aiming for a standard post details view and a reading progress bar. A minimalist newsletter sub box seems like a polished addition.\n\n\n**Implementing API Endpoints**\n\nI\'m now implementing the API endpoints in `server.ts`. I\'m starting with the `GET /api/posts` endpoint, using `fs.readdirSync` to scan the `/posts` folder and parse markdown files, and returning post metadata, sorted by date. Next, I\'ll build out `GET /api/posts/:id`, followed by the comments endpoints, `POST /api/posts/:id/comments` and `GET /api/posts/:id/comments`, storing comments in an in-memory object.\n\n\n"
    modified_response: "I will create the full-stack server `server.ts` using Express. The backend will parse the local markdown posts, extract frontmatter metadata, and expose REST API endpoints for fetching posts, retrieving post details, adding post comments, and managing newsletter subscriptions. It supports running hot in development via the Vite middleware and fallback to static paths in production."
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 8
      nanos: 303161383
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
