  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing CSS Imports**\n\nOkay, I\'ve started examining the CSS imports within `/src/index.css`. Specifically, I\'m focusing on the external font imports from Google Fonts. I see multiple fonts being imported: Inter, Space Grotesk, and JetBrains Mono, with different weights specified for the first two. Now, I will look into the implications for the overall project.\n\n\n**Considering React Image Components**\n\nI\'m now shifting gears, and I\'m really digging into the possibility of replicating Next.js image component functionality within our Vite React setup. My main thought is, how feasible is a standard React image component that behaves exactly like the Next.js `Image` component. I\'m exploring packages and techniques to implement semantic `<figure>` tags, strict alt text, and the Schema.org JSON-LD injection, to make it perfectly SEO compliant.\n\n\n**Evaluating Image Component Options**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into image optimization. The focus is to determine whether to build a custom `next/image` replacement using standard React components, or to implement a wrapper. Given Vite\'s environment, I am contemplating creating a `SeoImage.tsx` component to simulate `next/image`, which incorporates semantic `<figure>` tags, strict `alt` text validation, and Schema.org `ImageObject` JSON-LD injection for improved SEO.\n\n\n**Prioritizing User Intent**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on strictly interpreting the user\'s intent. My primary objective is to deliver exactly what was described: a minimalist news blog with specific features and aesthetic choices, adhering to the absolute ceiling of functionality defined by the prompt. I\'m focusing on file-based markdown content, placeholder articles, and the specified design choices. I am now considering how to provide requested code samples, and how to include specific next.js files as examples. I need to make sure the app meets all requirements, and explain it clearly!\n\n\n**Integrating Next.js Structure**\n\nI\'m now determining how to integrate the Next.js structure. Should I include a `/nextjs_version` directory with the necessary files, or integrate it directly into the main project? I can run a Next.js app on port 3000 by installing `next` and setting `\"dev\": \"next dev -p 3000\"` in `package.json`. However, I need to consider the Express server in `server.ts` that already reads markdown files, since Next.js can handle it.\n\n\n**Reconciling Framework Approaches**\n\nI\'m now wrestling with the core architectural decision. Do I fully migrate to Next.js, or maintain the existing Vite + React + Express structure and build Next.js-compatible components? Given the existing setup, including Vite, Tailwind CSS, and Express, I\'m leaning toward creating Next.js-like components within the React app. I can run next.js on a different port and provide examples!\n\n\n**Defining Component Goals**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the key objectives for the `SeoImage.tsx` component. It\'s crucial to create a React component that perfectly mirrors `next/image`, including semantic `<figure>` tags, strict `alt` text validation, responsive image sizing and attributes, and dynamic injection of Schema.org `ImageObject` JSON-LD for optimal SEO. This would then enable full generation of the described Next.js files and code. This will be very exciting.\n\n\n\n**Implementing Next.js Files**\n\nI am now planning the file structure to provide the requested `/page.tsx` file, ensuring perfect compatibility within our Vite + Express runtime. The approach will allow the preview application to operate without any issues, while also delivering the exact Next.js code and architecture the user requested. This preserves the runtime boundaries and respects the users\' intent perfectly!\n\n\n**Refining Image Component**\n\nI\'m now integrating Schema.org JSON-LD and semantic `<figure>` tags within the `SeoImage.tsx` component. The component includes image source (`src`), alt text (`alt`), width, height, and caption. I am focusing on ensuring SEO compliance and semantic correctness, and implementing a robust solution that is fully compliant with the request. I\'m excited about this.\n\n\n**Considering Placeholder Images**\n\nI\'m now contemplating how to handle image placeholders for the articles, and I\'m currently torn between generating elegant, high-contrast assets and using beautiful placeholders. I think an immediate focus could be on SVG inline backgrounds or clean editorial images from Unsplash. In the spirit of minimalist design, these options are perfect for fast loading times and great Core Web Vitals, and it\'ll all need to be integrated into `src/App.tsx`. I should implement a standard React router or single page view controller in `App.tsx` that links to each markdown post. A single-column news blog layout is required, and the user must be able to view posts with a header (\"THE MINIMALIST JOURNAL\"), and see the latest three posts, with the ability to tap on them to read them, in a gorgeous single-column article view.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 15
      nanos: 181082786
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
