  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Refining Data Handling**\n\nI\'ve successfully implemented the server-side exclusion of `content` from the index listing `/api/posts`, optimizing the returned data to include only `id`, `title`, `date`, `category`, `author`, `excerpt`, and `readTime`. My focus now shifts to incorporating a global search bar, as requested. The task now is to enable users to search posts by `title` and `excerpt`.\n\n\n**Evaluating Search Scope**\n\nI\'ve decided to re-evaluate the search scope. Initially, the plan was to optimize `/api/posts` by excluding \"content.\" However, after considering client-side search, I realize it\'s straightforward to include \"content\" in the response. This approach allows users to search across the entire post, including \"title,\" \"excerpt,\" and \"content\" in real-time. Given the small number of posts, the performance impact is negligible.\n\n\n**Optimizing API Response**\n\nI\'m considering a way to handle the `/api/posts` endpoint. I\'m thinking of returning the full `Post[]` data, including \"content.\" This simplifies client-side search across all fields (title, excerpt, content, category, author) and is efficient. The change is straightforward, and the performance impact on this size of site is negligible. I\'m focusing on lines 129-134 of `server.ts` to implement the required change. It looks like it is easily implemented!\n\n\n**Implementing Content Inclusion**\n\nI\'ve decided to proceed with including the full \"content\" field in the `/api/posts` response, always, since the number of posts is small. This eliminates the need for any complex server-side filtering or query parameters. I\'ve examined the post titles and confirmed the existing posts. I\'m focusing on modifying the `server.ts` file, specifically lines 129-134, to include the content directly within the posts array. I\'m confident this is the cleanest and most efficient solution for now.\n\n\n"
