  file_change {
    absolute_path_uri: "file:///posts/tech-advancements-2026.md"
    file_change_type: FILE_CHANGE_TYPE_CREATE
    replacement_chunks {
      replacement_content: "---\nid: tech-advancements-2026\ntitle: Why Web Creators are Going Back to Basics\ndate: 2026-05-18\ncategory: Trends\nauthor: Marcus Thorne\nexcerpt: In the past, websites became too complex and heavy. Today, programmers are going back to simpler, lightweight designs for a smoother, faster web.\nreadTime: 4 min read\n---\n\nOver the last ten years, making a website became incredibly complicated. Simple pages that once needed only basic text and a light layout were turned into complex networks of cloud servers, heavy data managers, and massive files that slow down your computer.\n\nToday, creators are learning that keeping things simple works much better. Super complicated cloud systems cost too much and make sites feel slow and sluggish for readers on mobile phones.\n\n### Why Simple is Better\n\nThe modern return to simple design focuses on clean code and smart, modern tools:\n\n*   **Simple setups**: Modern browsers and servers can read and display text files instantly without needing complex software configurations to start up.\n*   **Fast picture and text loaders**: New file bundlers organize website components in a fraction of a second, so nothing drags down the speed.\n*   **Local file databases**: Using simple folders of text files (just like our blog files!) is extremely fast and secure because there are no heavy cloud databases that can break down or stall your request.\n\n### Speed Comparison\n\nLet\'s look at the average loading time of a standard, heavy, over-complicated page compared to our simple, direct file-based page:\n\n| Style | Setup Difficulty | Load Speed on Phones | Phone Processing Delay |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Heavy cloud system** | Very High | Slow (1.8 to 3.2 seconds) | Laggy (Noticeable delay) |\n| **Simple file system (Ours)** | Very Low | **Instant (0.2 to 0.5 seconds)** | **Immediate (No delay)** |\n\n### Summary\n\nThe best website is always the simplest one that does the job. For reading newspapers and articles, serving simple, clear text is still the absolute best way to make readers happy.\n"
    }
    diff {
      end_line: 34
      unified_diff {
        lines {
          text: "---"
          type: UNIFIED_DIFF_LINE_TYPE_UNCHANGED
