  }
  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Designing the Highlighting Feature**\n\nI\'m currently focused on designing a highlighting feature for search results. Specifically, I want to bold the matched query text within post titles, excerpts, and content previews. It\'s important to clarify the definition of \'content previews\', and determine if it\'s the portion shown in the search result. I believe that careful design is paramount to this process.\n\n\n**Refining the Highlighting Function**\n\nI\'ve been refining the highlighting function, particularly for React. I will create `renderHighlightedText(text, query)`, handling empty queries by returning the original text. For non-empty queries, I\'ll split the text and map it to either highlighted or normal segments, using a simple bold tag since the instructions specify *bolding* the matched query text. Now I need to design the UI.\n\n\n**Implementing the Matching Logic**\n\nI\'m now implementing the matching logic for the highlighting feature, and have written the highlighting function. The function cleanly handles empty queries and leverages a regular expression for a case-insensitive match, using strong tags for the highlighting. However, a new question has arisen - how do we account for *content* matches within the content of each card? I\'m now contemplating a \"Matched in article:\" snippet to be displayed when the search query does not match the excerpt, but it does match the post content. I will look at post card rendering.\n\n\n**Defining the Presentation**\n\nI\'ve made great progress! I\'ve decided how to present the highlighted results in the UI. I will replace `{post.title}` and `{post.excerpt}` with highlighted versions using `{highlightText(post.title, searchQuery)}` and `{highlightText(post.excerpt, searchQuery)}`. I will add a \"Content match:\" preview under the excerpt if the query matches the full content, using snippets around 150 characters to fulfill the \"content previews\" requirement.\n\n\n**Crafting the Preview Logic**\n\nI\'ve been working on generating \"content previews\" to display when a match occurs within the post content. I\'m focusing on creating a `getContentPreview(content, query)` function, which finds a relevant snippet around the matched query within the content. This snippet is then highlighted and displayed with a clear label, enhancing the user experience. I\'ve designed logic to handle edge cases, such as the start and end of the content.\n\n\n**Adding the Sharing Features**\n\nI am now adding social media sharing functionality to the single post view. I will include a sharing button block with Twitter/X, Facebook, LinkedIn, and a \"Copy Link\" option. I\'m using icons from `lucide-react`. I think it\'s a beautifully designed and functional sharing bar with buttons!\n\n\n**Constructing the Sharing Links**\n\nNow, I\'m focusing on crafting the social media sharing links. I\'ve double-checked the imports to confirm the availability of necessary icons. My approach is to construct the share URLs directly. For Twitter/X, I\'ll use `https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?url=${encodeURIComponent(shareUrl)}&text=${encodeURIComponent(activePost.title)}`, and for Facebook, it\'s `https://www.facebook.com/sharer/sharer.php?u=${encodeURIComponent(shareUrl)}`. LinkedIn will use `https://www.linkedin.com/sharing/share-offsite/?url=${encodeURIComponent(shareUrl)}`. I\'m developing a `getShareUrl()` function that falls back to a production/dev site URL in case `window` is unavailable or on `localhost`. I\'ll style this sharing section to be clean and newspaper-themed.\n\n\n**Styling the Sharing Section**\n\nI\'m now focusing on styling the social media sharing section to be clean and newspaper-themed. I\'m using Tailwind classes for a visually appealing presentation. The section will have a subtle border and use a combination of text styles for the labels and buttons. I\'ve centered the content with a gap.\n\n\n**Refining the Link Generation**\n\nI\'m now refining the social sharing functionality, specifically the link generation. I\'m focusing on ensuring all the share links and the copy functionality work as expected and are fully functional. I\'m creating the copy link function which is triggered upon button click. I will also incorporate a state variable to show a \"Copied!\" message for a brief period after the copy operation is successful, with a small timeout. I\'m planning to thoroughly test all sharing options across various browsers and devices.\n\n\n\n**Designing the Admin Panel**\n\nI\'m now designing the admin panel, with a strong focus on security and usability. I will begin by creating a dedicated login view with clear, simple credentials. For login, I\'ll need a unique username and password. Let\'s make it highly secure yet easy to read: the username is `admin` and the password is `zeronews2026`. I envision an interface to view all posts, analyze comments, view or manage the newsletter subscriber list, and create or update posts. The panel must be fully responsive and visually clean, ensuring a smooth experience.\n\n\n**Implementing the Backend API**\n\nI am now implementing backend API endpoints for full CRUD operations on posts and retrieving newsletter signups, which will fully satisfy our goals! I\'m creating secure endpoints in `/server.ts` for listing email signups, creating, updating, and deleting posts. This includes endpoints for both the post data itself and the newsletter subscribers. I am protecting these endpoints with the Admin Panel access control, focusing on best practices for security. This allows for a completely integrated, real-world experience, and complete management of posts and subscribers.\n\n\n**Defining the File System**\n\nI\'m now determining how to handle file system interactions. I\'ll utilize `fs.writeFileSync` to modify or create posts, confirming its functionality. I will also read the `/posts` directory\'s contents to confirm the frontmatter and Markdown structure. I\'m verifying the parsed post\'s fields and am ready to start implementing write operations in `/server.ts`. I will figure out how to format the Markdown content when saving a post.\n\n\n**Adding the Admin Endpoints**\n\nI am now adding the admin API endpoints to `/server.ts` to enable full CRUD operations for posts and newsletter subscriber management. I\'ve focused on ensuring security by validating input and file paths. I\'ve added a GET endpoint to retrieve newsletter signups and a POST endpoint for creating new posts, complete with the appropriate data validation. I am writing functions to write to `/posts`, ensuring robust, secure file handling and I\'m adding validation for all provided data.\n\n\n**Constructing the Post Update**\n\nNow, I\'m constructing the POST update functionality. I\'m focusing on ensuring all the share links and the copy functionality work as expected and are fully functional. I\'ve successfully implemented the POST endpoint, and I\'m currently working to fully implement the PUT endpoint. I\'m focusing on incorporating validation to ensure that the supplied data has the correct format and content, especially with updates.\n\n\n**Implementing the Update Endpoint**\n\nI\'m now implementing the update endpoint to fully handle the PUT method. I will read the existing post, and then merge the provided data. I am adding validation to ensure that the supplied data has the correct format and content, especially with updates. I am verifying the updated file content, including `id`, `title`, and `content`, to ensure the update goes as planned.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 21
      nanos: 972149892
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
