  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Considering Share Button Placement**\n\nI\'ve been exploring optimal placement for the share buttons. I\'ve concluded that putting them directly below the header or just after the primary text content would be elegant and clean, above all else. This seems to offer a balance between visibility and avoiding clutter.\n\n\n**Implementing Share Button Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the share button directly below the header, right where I decided it belonged! I\'ve placed the share bar within the header block, just before the author information. I\'m now writing out the social share block code, and adding the styles. I think this will greatly enhance the user experience by making sharing immediate and effortless!\n\n\n**Refining Social Share Display**\n\nI\'m currently focused on refining the social share display, specifically integrating the share icons with the author information, so it flows better visually. I am adding a \"share\" button next to the author information, rather than a standalone section at this stage, so I can test integration and impact.\n\n\n**Adding Social Share Functionality**\n\nI\'m now implementing the Facebook and X (formerly Twitter) share functionality, directly into the \"share\" button next to author information. I\'ve focused on ensuring the links accurately capture the current page URL and article title for the shares. The buttons are styled for consistency.\n\n\n**Adding More Social Options**\n\nI\'ve just added LinkedIn and a \"copy link\" feature to the share options! I\'m now providing more sharing choices; each has a clear, concise icon. The copy link functionality makes it easy for users to share the direct article URL anywhere they choose, going beyond established social networks. I\'m aiming for flexibility and user control.\n\n\n"
