    thinking: "**Refining Comment Filtering**\n\nI\'ve realized a critical detail: new comments, initially unapproved, need special handling. The previous code was adding them directly. Now, I must ensure unapproved comments (those with `approved: false` or `pendingModeration: true`) are filtered out of the main comment display until approved by an administrator. This ensures a clean and moderated user experience.\n\n\n**Implementing Success Notification**\n\nI\'ve just added a success notification. When a comment is submitted for moderation, a friendly message will appear within the form area. It will inform the user their comment is pending approval. This feedback improves the user experience during comment submission.\n\n\n**Confirming Clean Moderation**\n\nI\'ve confirmed the recent code successfully handles pending moderation. Submitted comments are no longer prematurely added to the main display. Instead, submission clears the form and signals the user with a temporary \"success\" notification. It fits perfectly with the backend filtering. Next, I\'m shifting focus to designing the admin panel\'s frontend, starting with the navigation layout.\n\n\n**Expanding Admin Views**\n\nI\'m expanding the `adminSubView` state to include \"edit\", \"comments\", \"images\", and \"emails\", in addition to \"list\" and \"new\". This will provide comprehensive admin panel functionality. I\'m also preparing to fetch and store a `commentsReview` state containing all system comments, to be managed and moderated. The admin panel frontend design is now evolving!\n\n\n**Defining API Loaders**\n\nI\'m now defining API loaders for admin view data. I\'ve set up `fetchAllComments`, `fetchUploadedImages`, and a function for email logs to fetch data on login or sub-view load. This approach is cleaner and more scalable than previous state initialization. Each fetch handles errors.\n\n\n**Orchestrating Data Fetching**\n\nI\'ve structured a useEffect hook to load data responsively within the admin panel.  Comments, images, and email logs are now fetched conditionally, based on the `adminSubView` state and `adminLoggedIn` status. This prevents redundant loads. The logic looks really clean, and I can trigger actions after comments are approved or deleted now! I\'m now thinking about what actions an admin can take on comments.\n\n\n**Defining Comment Actions**\n\nI\'m now implementing the actions an admin can take on comments! I\'ve defined `handleApproveComment` to update the comment\'s `approved` state optimistically. I\'ve also written `handleRejectComment` to confirm deletion before removing the comment. Both update the view if a comment is currently selected.\n\n\n**Designing Image Upload**\n\nI\'m now focusing on image uploads. I\'m considering a drag-and-drop area versus a standard file input for user selection. I\'m leaning toward the drag-and-drop. I\'ve begun an upload function that validates file types and sizes. Currently, it reads the file using `FileReader` and sets up error handling to display relevant messages to the user.\n\n\n**Implementing Image Uploading**\n\nI\'ve just finished the image upload logic! When a user selects or drags and drops, the function validates the file and reads it as a data URL. It then sends this data to the server, parses the response, and updates the state with the uploaded image\'s details if successful. I\'ve also implemented error handling, informing the user if upload fails or if there are connection problems. Finally, I\'ve added a `finally` block to reset the `imageUploading` state.\n\nI\'m now thinking about an interface for the uploaded images.\n\n\n"
